(meta (title "Lists and tables")
      (date 2026-09-25))

A list can be written the way Markdown or Org writes one, nested by how far
an item is indented:

@read-list{
- flour, water and salt;
- yeast,
  - fresh,
  - or dried;
- and time.
}

or command by command, which is how a numbered one is asked for:

@itemlist[#:style ordered]{
  @item{Mix.}
  @item{Wait.}
  @item{Bake.}
}

A table is written as Org writes one. A rule under the first row makes it the
head, and a column that is mostly numbers is set to the right:

@read-table[#:caption "Pancakes, for four"]{
| Ingredient | Amount | Unit |
|------------+--------+------|
| flour      |    2.5 | dl   |
| milk       |      6 | dl   |
| eggs       |      3 |      |
| salt       |    0.5 | tsp  |
}

And a quotation:

@blockquote{
  The whole recipe is on @link["../recipes/pancakes.html"]{its own page}.
}

@include["../_parts/signature.sz"]
